## Prompt:
How can Datomic be used to build a personal collection inventory system which would keep track of the physical locations of objects and connect them to digital photos of the objects in a flexible manner where movements of the physical objects can be like a library checkout?

## Response:
Datomic is a strong fit for this because two of its core properties map directly onto what you need: everything is stored as immutable time-stamped facts (datoms), and the schema is flexible/additive rather than rigid tables. That gets you "checkout history" almost for free, and lets photos attach to objects in loose, evolving ways instead of a fixed one-to-one column.

Here's how I'd design it.

## Why Datomic's model fits

- **Datoms are `[entity attribute value tx added?]`** — every fact is timestamped and never overwritten, only retracted-and-asserted. So "location" isn't just a field you update; each update leaves a permanent trail.
- **`d/history`, `d/as-of`, `d/since`** let you query "where was this vase on March 1st?" or "show every place this item has ever lived" without you building any of that bookkeeping yourself.
- **Flexible, additive schema** means an item can have zero, one, or many photos, tags, or provenance documents without schema migrations — you just add datoms.
- **Datalog** makes "many-to-many, evolving relationships" (item ↔ photo, item ↔ location history) easy to query in ways SQL joins get awkward for.

## Core schema

```clojure
;; Items
{:db/ident :item/id
 :db/valueType :db.type/uuid
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one
 :db/unique :db.unique/identity}

{:db/ident :item/name
 :db/valueType :db.type/string
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :item/category
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :item/current-location
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
 ;; kept as a "current pointer" for cheap lookups;
 ;; full history comes from Datomic's log, not from this alone

;; Locations (self-referential hierarchy: room > shelf > box)
{:db/ident :location/id
 :db/valueType :db.type/uuid
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one
 :db/unique :db.unique/identity}

{:db/ident :location/name
 :db/valueType :db.type/string
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :location/parent
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :location/type
 :db/valueType :db.type/keyword
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
 ;; :room :shelf :box :person :external :in-transit

;; Photos
{:db/ident :photo/id
 :db/valueType :db.type/uuid
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one
 :db/unique :db.unique/identity}

{:db/ident :photo/uri
 :db/valueType :db.type/string
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :photo/items
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many}
 ;; many-to-many: one photo can show several items (e.g. a shelf shot),
 ;; one item can have several photos

{:db/ident :photo/role
 :db/valueType :db.type/keyword
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
 ;; :primary :detail :damage :provenance :packaging

;; Movement / checkout events — this is your "library circulation" record
{:db/ident :movement/item
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :movement/from
