## Prompt:
How can containers be modeled, for example a box of magazines, articles in magazines?

## Response:
Containers are the perfect stress-test for the schema I sketched — and the good insight is that in Datomic, **a container doesn't need to be a new kind of thing**. A box is just an item that other items can point to as their location. That single move (relaxing "location" to mean "any entity, fixed or portable") gives you arbitrary nesting for free. Articles-in-magazines is a *different* relationship though — not physical containment but bibliographic composition — so it deserves its own pattern. Let me cover both.

## Key decision: containers are items, not a separate type

Instead of keeping `:location` (fixed infrastructure: rooms, shelves) and `:item` (things you own) as separate worlds, let `:item/current-location` be a ref to **either**. A box is an item with a category of `:box`; other items point to it the same way they'd point to a shelf.

```clojure
{:db/ident :item/current-location
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
 ;; value may be a Location entity (room/shelf/building)
 ;; OR another Item entity (a box, a folder, a crate)

{:db/ident :item/category
 :db/valueType :db.type/keyword
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
 ;; :magazine :box :figurine :book :folder ...
```

No schema-level type-checking is needed — Datomic refs don't care what's on the other end. You just need a convention (`:location/type` present = "this is fixed infrastructure"; absent = "this is a portable item that happens to hold things").

**Why this matters:** moving the box moves everything inside it *implicitly*. You only write one movement datom for the box; the magazines' `:item/current-location` still points at the box, which is now elsewhere. You never have to cascade-update contents.

## Resolving "where is this, really?"

Since a magazine's `:item/current-location` might point to a box, which points to a shelf, which points to a room, you need a recursive walk to find the physical place. Datalog rules handle this cleanly:

```clojure
[[(contained-in ?item ?place)
  [?item :item/current-location ?place]]
 [(contained-in ?item ?place)
  [?item :item/current-location ?mid]
  (contained-in ?mid ?place)]

 [(fixed-location ?item ?loc)
  (contained-in ?item ?loc)
  [?loc :location/type _]]]  ;; stop once we hit real infrastructure
```

```clojure
(d/q '[:find ?item-name ?room-name
       :in $ %
       :where
       [?item :item/name ?item-name]
       (fixed-location ?item ?loc)
       [?loc :location/name ?room-name]]
     db rules)
```

This tells you "the *Life* magazine is, transitively, on the office bookshelf" even though its direct pointer is to a box.

## Listing contents recursively

```clojure
[[(contents ?container ?thing)
  [?thing :item/current-location ?container]]
 [(contents ?container ?thing)
  [?mid :item/current-location ?container]
  (contents ?mid ?thing)]]
```

```clojure
(d/q '[:find ?name
       :in $ % ?box
       :where
       (contents ?box ?thing)
       [?thing :item/name ?name]]
     db rules box-eid)
```

Gives you every magazine (or nested sub-box) inside a box, at any depth.

## Preventing cycles

Because containment is just refs, nothing stops you from accidentally putting box A inside box B inside box A. Enforce this with a **transaction function** rather than app-level checking, so it's atomic and can't be bypassed:

```clojure
{:db/ident :fns/set-location
 :db/fn (d/function
         '{:lang :clojure
           :params [db item new-loc]
           :code (let [rules '[[(contains? ?c ?x) [?x :item/current-location ?c]]
                                [(contains? ?c ?x) [?y :item/current-location ?c]
                                                    (contains? ?y ?x)]]]
                   (if (seq (d/q '[:find ?item :in $ % ?item ?loc
                                    :where (contains? ?item ?loc)]
                                  db rules item new-loc))
                     (throw (ex-info "Cycle: item already contains destination" {}))
                     [[:db/add item :item/current-location new-loc]]))})}
```

Then all moves go through `[:fns/set-location item-id new-loc-id]` instead of a raw `:db/add`.

## Magazines and articles: composition, not containment

An article isn't a separately-movable physical object — it never has its own location, it just *is part of* a magazine. That's a different relationship, closer to a **component** than a container:

```clojure
{:db/ident :article/magazine
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :article/title
 :db/valueType :db.type/string
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :article/page-start
 :db/valueType :db.type/long
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :article/page-end
 :db/valueType :db.type/long
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :article/authors
 :db/valueType :db.type/string
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many}

{:db/ident :article/topics
 :db/valueType :db.type/keyword
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many}
```

Optionally mark the reverse side as a component so retracting a magazine cleans up its articles automatically:

```clojure
{:db/ident :magazine/articles
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many
 :db/isComponent true}
```

`:db/isComponent` is the right tool specifically because an article has no independent existence or location — it's owned by exactly one magazine and should vanish with it. (Don't do this for box-contents refs — a magazine still exists conceptually if you empty the box, so that relationship should *not* be a component.)

## Photos at any granularity

Since `:photo/items` is just `:db.type/ref` with no type constraint, it can point at a box, a magazine, or a specific article — a photo of one page:

```clojure
[{:db/id "photo1"
  :photo/uri "s3://collection/img4471.jpg"
  :photo/role :detail
  :photo/items [[:article/id article-uuid]]}]
```

or a photo showing the whole box's contents:

```clojure
{:photo/items [[:item/id box-uuid] [:item/id mag1-uuid] [:item/id mag2-uuid]]}
```

## Checking out a whole box

Because contents point to the container rather than a resolved location, "loan out the whole box" is one movement — no per-item updates:

```clojure
[{:db/id "movement"
  :movement/item [:item/id box-uuid]
  :movement/from [:location/id shelf-uuid]
  :movement/to [:location/id friend-loc-uuid]
  :movement/reason :loaned-out
  :movement/due-date #inst "2026-10-20"}
 {:db/id [:item/id box-uuid]
  :item/current-location [:location/id friend-loc-uuid]}]
```

Query "what's currently at my friend's house" via the `contents` rule against the box, and it correctly reports every magazine inside — their own `:item/current-location` datoms never had to change.

## Summary of the pattern

| Relationship | Mechanism | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Box → shelf/room | ref via `:item/current-location` to a `Location` | fixed infrastructure |
| Magazine → box | ref via `:item/current-location` to another `Item` | portable containment, recursive |
| Article → magazine | `:article/magazine` ref, `:db/isComponent` | bibliographic composition, no independent location |
| Photo → box/magazine/article | `:photo/items` ref, untyped | flexible many-to-many at any granularity |

