## Prompt:
How can connections between information be modeled, for example, a relationship between a book chapter and a magazine article?

## Response:
This is a different problem than containment. A box *owns* what's inside it, and an article *belongs to* one magazine — those are ownership/composition relationships, cleanly expressed as plain refs. But "this book chapter cites that magazine article" is a **peer-to-peer relationship between two independent things**, and it needs its own attributes (what kind of relationship? when did you notice it? how confident are you?). A plain `:db/valueType :db.type/ref` can't carry that extra information — so you reify the relationship itself as an entity.

## Why you can't just add a ref attribute

You could write:

```clojure
{:db/ident :chapter/references
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many}
```

This works for one relationship type but breaks down fast: you can't say *why* two things are related, you can't add a note, you can't distinguish "quotes directly" from "covers the same topic," and every new relationship type needs a new attribute. For a personal collection where you'll constantly discover new kinds of connections, you want the type of connection to be **data**, not schema.

## The pattern: a reified `Connection` entity

Same trick as `:movement` for physical location — turn the relationship into its own first-class entity so it can carry arbitrary attributes:

```clojure
{:db/ident :connection/id
 :db/valueType :db.type/uuid
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one
 :db/unique :db.unique/identity}

{:db/ident :connection/from
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :connection/to
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :connection/type
 :db/valueType :db.type/ref     ;; ref to a relation-type entity, not a bare keyword — see below
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :connection/note
 :db/valueType :db.type/string
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :connection/confidence
 :db/valueType :db.type/keyword
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
 ;; :certain :probable :speculative

{:db/ident :connection/page-ref
 :db/valueType :db.type/string
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
 ;; "ch. 3, p. 47" — where in ?from the connection is made
```

Because `:connection/from` and `:connection/to` are untyped refs, they can point at *any* entity regardless of kind — a book chapter, a magazine article, a photo, even another connection if you want meta-relationships later. This is the same flexibility win as `:photo/items`.

## Making relation types data, not keywords

Rather than hardcoding `:references`/`:inspired-by`/`:contradicts` as raw keywords scattered through your app, model them as entities. This lets you add new relationship types at runtime, attach a human-readable description, and mark whether a type is symmetric:

```clojure
{:db/ident :relation-type/id
 :db/valueType :db.type/keyword
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one
 :db/unique :db.unique/identity}

{:db/ident :relation-type/label
 :db/valueType :db.type/string
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}

{:db/ident :relation-type/symmetric?
 :db/valueType :db.type/boolean
 :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/one}
```

Seed data:

```clojure
[{:relation-type/id :references
  :relation-type/label "References / cites"
  :relation-type/symmetric? false}
 {:relation-type/id :quotes
  :relation-type/label "Quotes directly"
  :relation-type/symmetric? false}
 {:relation-type/id :inspired-by
  :relation-type/label "Inspired by"
  :relation-type/symmetric? false}
 {:relation-type/id :same-topic
  :relation-type/label "Covers the same topic"
  :relation-type/symmetric? true}
 {:relation-type/id :contradicts
  :relation-type/label "Contradicts"
  :relation-type/symmetric? true}]
```

## Book chapters (mirroring the article/magazine pattern)

```clojure
