Same reasoning as `:magazine/articles` — a chapter has no independent existence outside its book, so it's a component.

## Creating a connection

```clojure
[{:db/id "conn1"
  :connection/id #uuid "..."
  :connection/from [:chapter/id chapter-uuid]
  :connection/to [:article/id article-uuid]
  :connection/type :references
  :connection/note "Chapter's footnote 12 cites this article's survey data"
  :connection/confidence :certain
  :connection/page-ref "p. 203"}]
```

The transaction's `:db/txInstant` already gives you "when I recorded this connection" for free, same as with movements — no need for a separate timestamp attribute unless the user wants to backdate it.

## Querying: outgoing, incoming, and direction-agnostic

Because `from`/`to` is directional, "what connects to X" needs a rule that checks both sides:

```clojure
[[(connected ?a ?b)
  [?c :connection/from ?a]
  [?c :connection/to ?b]]
 [(connected ?a ?b)
  [?c :connection/from ?b]
  [?c :connection/to ?a]]]
```

```clojure
(d/q '[:find ?title ?type-label
       :in $ % ?chapter
       :where
       (connected ?chapter ?other)
       [?c :connection/from ?chapter]
       [?c :connection/to ?other]
       [?c :connection/type ?type]
       [?type :relation-type/label ?type-label]
       [?other :article/title ?title]]
     db rules chapter-eid)
```

Filter by type (e.g., only contradictions):

```clojure
(d/q '[:find ?from-name ?to-name
       :where
       [?c :connection/type :contradicts]
       [?c :connection/from ?f]
       [?c :connection/to ?t]
       (or [?f :article/title ?from-name] [?f :chapter/title ?from-name])
       (or [?t :article/title ?to-name] [?t :chapter/title ?to-name])]
     db)
```

`or` clauses like this are exactly where the flexible, untyped-entity approach pays off — the query doesn't care whether `?f` resolves to a chapter or an article, it just asks each possible attribute.

## Graph traversal: multi-hop connections

Since connections form a graph, you can walk transitively — e.g., "everything within 2 hops of this article":

```clojure
[[(reachable ?a ?b 1)
  (connected ?a ?b)]
 [(reachable ?a ?b ?n)
  (connected ?a ?mid)
  (reachable ?mid ?b ?n2)
  [(dec ?n) ?n2]]]  ;; simplified — Datomic rules don't do arithmetic guards natively,
                     ;; typically you'd just write reachable/2 without depth-limiting
                     ;; or bound it procedurally in application code
```

In practice, unbounded transitive closure in Datalog can be expensive/unwieldy for depth-limited graph walks; it's often simpler to do a **breadth-first walk in application code**, calling `d/q` for direct neighbors at each hop and stopping at your desired depth. Datomic is good at "give me all direct connections of X repeatedly" — orchestrating the hop limit in Clojure/whatever host language is usually cleaner than encoding depth counting in Datalog rules.

## Tying it back to physical objects

A connection isn't restricted to "informational" entities — you can link a book chapter to the physical magazine item too, not just an abstract `:article` entity, if what you care about is "I own the physical copy this chapter refers to":

```clojure
{:connection/from [:chapter/id chapter-uuid]
 :connection/to [:item/id physical-magazine-uuid]
 :connection/type :references}
```

Because every entity in Datomic — physical item, location, photo, article, chapter, connection — lives in the same undifferentiated entity space and refs don't care what they point to, this whole system (physical tracking, containment, checkout, informational cross-references) is really **one graph**, just queried with different rule sets depending on what question you're asking. That uniformity is the main payoff of using Datomic here rather than a schema-per-relationship relational design.

